Fix folder-source drag disable, bookmark reorder direction, and horizontal resize line

- Folder entries remain valid drop targets but are no longer draggable sources. This is explicitly marked DISABLED ONLY; the previous draggable assignments and underlying drag handler remain in the code. Leaf bookmarks inside folders and nested subfolders remain draggable.
- Permit native drag initiation from bookmark leaf rows in folder popovers and nested submenus.
- Correct same-parent bookmark reordering: preserve the target insertion index when calling Chrome's bookmarks.move rather than decrementing it twice. This fixes drops intended to move an earlier item below a later one.
- Disable the horizontal docked strip's bottom/top resize-line gesture, including its separate outside hit buffer. Vertical-mode resizing remains unchanged. The original grip markup/handler remains and is marked as disabled only.
- Existing overlay-drag behavior closes open menus at the movement threshold. Tab tooltips stay outside the horizontal/vertical tab strip.

Validation: edited JavaScript files pass syntax parsing and same-parent reorder cases were checked against the insertion-index calculation. No live Chrome UI test was run.

Update 2026-10-28 — bookmark overflow reorder and window preview

- Resolve bookmark drop targets against the current Chrome bookmark tree before moving. The horizontal More (») menu renders only the overflow slice, so its displayed row indices are not authoritative for same-folder reorder operations.
- Show saved FullNav window names in the horizontal other-window preview and retain a hover tooltip in compact icon mode.
- Chrome's public extension windows API does not expose the native Chrome taskbar/window-preview naming control; these labels remain FullNav labels and do not change page titles.

Validation: JavaScript syntax parsing passed for the edited scripts, and representative same-parent reorder directions were checked against Chrome's index-adjustment behavior. No live Chrome UI test was run.
- Clarify the rename menu as FullNav-only: the public extension API cannot rename Chrome's native taskbar/window-preview title.

Follow-up 2026-10-28 — bookmark drag and overflow correction

- Reverted the unrequested window-preview label changes and restored the previous window-name menu wording and preview appearance.
- Resolve reorder destination from the live target bookmark and its actual parent; calculate the target slot from the live sibling array and translate it into Chrome's insertion-boundary index. This avoids relying on cached indices or parent IDs from the horizontal » slice and lazy submenus.
- Prefer the last valid dragover target where native drop events are retargeted in nested/overflow menus. Suppress a trailing click only for the dragged and dropped bookmark IDs, so a reorder cannot immediately trigger either bookmark's open action.

Validation: JavaScript syntax parsing passed for content.js and background.js. Representative upward, downward, end-position, and no-op sibling reorder cases passed simulation. No live Chrome UI test was run.

Follow-up 2026-10-28 — bookmark popup layering and drag-open folders

- Place bookmark-folder and overflow menus beside the panel/bookmark bar when viewport space permits. They choose the clearer side of the anchor; if no clear side fits, the overlay and bookmark bar remain layered above them.
- Keep hover tooltips above the overlay normally, but hide them over modal backdrops. While bookmark edit/add dialogs are open, folder menus and nested submenus are non-interactive and remain below the dialog.
- Re-enable dragging folder entries and nested folders as bookmark sources. Dragging an internal bookmark over a folder in the root bar, overflow menu, or nested submenu opens that folder so its contents can be targeted.
- Remove the blue before/after marker on the tab bar specifically for bookmark drags; tab-to-tab drag indicators are unchanged.

Validation: syntax parsing passed for content.js, fn-menu.js, fn-bookmarks.js, and fn-css.js. Static assertions confirmed folder drag guards are gone and drag-hover listeners are present. No live Chrome UI test was run.
